This print pictures the geisha named Nanako from the Yoshichō geisha quarters of Tokyo. One of the two prints contributed by the artist to Shin Nigao.
&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;SHIN NIGAO (NEW ACTOR PORTRAITS)&lt;br&gt;
During the Taishō period (1912-1926), many urban Japanese youth were drawn away from kabuki to more modern forms of entertainment, especially Western imports like cinema and baseball. One notable effort to revive interest in actor prints was the publication of Shin Nigao 新似顔 (New Actor Portraits) magazine in 1915. This art magazine was a collaboration among several well-known Japanese artists, including Natori Shunsen (1886-1960), Ishii Hakutei (1882-1958), and Yamamura Toyonari (1885-1942). The general purpose of Shin Nigao was to advertise for the kabuki theater, but it also gave the artists a chance to express themselves in new ways. A total of 77 prints were published in the magazine over five issues, with each of the first four issues featuring 14 small woodblock prints and the fifth, and last, the &quot;Coronation&quot; issue, featuring 21 prints, including five portraits of officials and entertainers associated with the November 1915 enthronement ceremonies of Yoshihito (Emperor Taishō).

This print is one of fifty prints from the first part of the three part series One Hundred Victories, One Hundred Laughs, which presented comic, often racist, parodies of the Chinese enemy. The text on the prints was written by Koppi Dōjin (Master, Skin and Bones), a pseudonym used by the newspaper writer Nishimori Takeki (1861–1913).&lt;br&gt;
The Japanese dragonfly stands over the Chinese pig, while the three bees representing Russia, England and France buzz around China&#39;s head.  By the time this print was released in December 1894, Japan was in control of Port Arthur and the Liaodong Peninsula. A few years after the war ended, Russia, England and France all obtained leases of various strategic ports from a weak China.&lt;br&gt; 
Depiction of Japan as a Dragonfly - Japan was not always named Japan. Japanese legend has it that an Emperor was bitten by a horsefly which, in turn, was eaten by a dragonfly. The Emperor honored the dragonfly by naming what is now Japan “Akitsushima” which, during that time, translated to “Isle of the Dragonfly”.

This print is one of fifty prints from the first part of the three part series One Hundred Victories, One Hundred Laughs, which presented comic, often racist, parodies of the Chinese enemy. The text on the prints was written by Koppi Dōjin (Master, Skin and Bones), a pseudonym used by the newspaper writer Nishimori Takeki (1861–1913).&lt;br&gt; 

In the print Chinese troops fall to the ground in fear as a Japanese soldier in guise of the thunder god appears, with his drums and a lightning flash.&lt;br&gt;

Although the Pescadores were garrisoned by 15 Chinese regular battalions (5,000 men) and defended by the recently-completed Hsi-tai coastal defense battery, the Japanese met very little resistance during the landing operation as the defenders were demoralized. It took the Japanese only three days to secure the islands.

One of fourteen prints by the artist that appeared in the magazine Shin Nigao whose purpose was to advertise the Kabuki theater and renew interest in actor prints. This print appeared in the 4th issue published in October 1915 and portrays the actor Ichikawa Yaozō VII in the role of Kiyomasa in the production of Zōho momoyama monogatari at the Hongō-za..
