Pictured is the actor Bandō Hikosaburō VI in the role of Genba from the play Sugawara&#39;s Secrets of Calligraphy 菅原傅授手習鑑 staged at the Ichimura-za, Tokyo on October 31, 1915. The artist contributed a single print to the  magazine Shin Nigao whose purpose was to advertise the Kabuki theater and renew interest in actor prints.
&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;SHIN NIGAO (NEW ACTOR PORTRAITS)&lt;br&gt;
During the Taishō period (1912-1926), many urban Japanese youth were drawn away from kabuki to more modern forms of entertainment, especially Western imports like cinema and baseball. One notable effort to revive interest in actor prints was the publication of Shin Nigao 新似顔 (New Actor Portraits) magazine in 1915. This art magazine was a collaboration among several well-known Japanese artists, including Natori Shunsen (1886-1960), Ishii Hakutei (1882-1958), and Yamamura Toyonari (1885-1942). The general purpose of Shin Nigao was to advertise for the kabuki theater, but it also gave the artists a chance to express themselves in new ways. A total of 77 prints were published in the magazine over five issues, with each of the first four issues featuring 14 small woodblock prints and the fifth, and last, the &quot;Coronation&quot; issue, featuring 21 prints, including five portraits of officials and entertainers associated with the November 1915 enthronement ceremonies of Yoshihito (Emperor Taishō).

The court dancer Seikanji Utako is dressed to perform the dance Gosechi no Maihime at the coronation ceremony of the Taishō emperor. According to a newspaper report in the The Straits Times (Singapore), 28 August 1915, &quot;In regard to the entertainments to be given on the first day of the Coronation, the ancient &#39;quintuple dance&#39; known as Gosechi-no-Mai will constitute, according to the Coronation Committee the most prominent part of the programme. The origin of this dance is said to be traceable as far back as the days of Emperor Temmu (697 a.d.). The ladies who will perform the dance next November at the Coronation celebration have been selected from among the unmarried daughters of the descendants of ancient courtiers, to the number of eight, of whom three form the reserve. The ladies vary in age from 19 to 23 years, and will appear dressed in ancient costumes.&quot; One of seven prints contributed by the artist to the magazine Shin Nigao.
