The first of eight prints in the series The Eight Views of Japan (Nihon Hakkei) published by Uchida Woodblock Printing Company sometime in the 1950s.  This print, depicting Chogosonshi-ji Temple on a moonlit night, was originally published in 1941 as part of the series Scenes of Sacred and Historic Places. 
&lt;br&gt;This series reused eight previously issued images from the 1941 series Scenes of Sacred and Historic Places (Seichi Shiseki Meisho) and reissued them in a portfolio of prints titled Eight Views of Japan. The table of contents accompanying the entire series notes that the prints are &quot;Selections from Prints of Places of Historic and Scenic Interest&quot; (a.k.a. Scenes of Sacred and Historic Places) and that it contains &quot;2 each of four seasons.&quot;
&lt;br&gt;Mt. Shigi-san, 437 meters in elevation, is located at the southern end of the Ikoma Mountains lying in the northwest of Nara Prefecture. In the middle of the mountain is Chogosonshi-ji Temple, usually referred to as &quot;Shigisan&quot; or &quot;Shigi-no-bishamon-san.&quot; The temple&#39;s principal deity, Vaisravana (Bishamonten in Japanese), brings good luck and protection to those who come to worship. The temple owns one of the most important illustrated handscrolls in Japanese art history, the 12th-century Shigisan Engi Emaki (Illustrated Handscroll, Legend of Mount Shigi).

Depicting a scene from the Noh play Ugetsu by the playwright Komparu Zenchiku (1405-c. 1470).&lt;br&gt;
Play synopsis (A Guide to No, P.G. O&#39;Neill, Hinoki Shoten, 1929, p. 202-203.) - Arriving at Sumiyoshi on a pilgrimage to the shrine there, the priest Saigyō seeks shelter in the house of an old couple. They gladly give him lodging when he succeeds in providing a beginning to the second part of a poem which the old man composed about a dispute between him and his wife: she, liking to watch the moon, wants the eaves of the house left unthatched but he enjoys hearing the sound of the rain on the thatching there. In the second part of the play, Sumiyoshi Miyōjin, the patron god of poetry, takes possession of an old keeper of the shrine to speak of poetry and dance.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Illustrations of Noh is Kōgyo&#39;s first series of Noh theater prints issued between 1897 and 1904. Its 261 prints include 221 depicting Noh plays, 27 depicting kyōgen plays and 13 miscellaneous prints, e.g. props, costumes and five illustrated tables of content. The prints show the primary actor (shite) often accompanied by one or two other performers (known as the waki, tsure, or aikyōgen), stage props, and sometimes the larger milieu including musicians or stage architecture. In the right margin Kōgyo gives the title of the play, and for Noh plays only, a list of the roles and a brief synopsis of the story.
