&lt;br&gt;This didactic series was published between October 1885 and November 1890 and featured a long list of heroes and heroines, from antiquity to contemporary times, who were regarded as standards of moral leadership and self-realization. Originally envisioned as a 50 print series, it would grow to 58 prints. Seven artists contributed to the series and upon its completion the publisher, Matsuki Heikichi, was singled out for special recognition by the government for having sponsored such &quot;noble subject matter.&quot;

Print number 40 (四十) as shown in the series&#39; Table of Contents, although not numbered on the print. One of 16 prints designed by Toshikata for the series Instructive Models of Lofty Ambition, picturing Ōishi Kuranosuke Yoshio (1659-1703) leader of the 47 Ronin surrounded by female entertainers under a full moon. This scene references Ōishi&#39;s ruse of living the high life with the geisha of Kyoto, while he was plotting his revenge on Lord Kira, responsible for the death of his master Lord Asano.  Ōishi&#39;s actions in avenging his master and his subsequent suicide, as ordered by the shogunate, was seen as ideal samurai behavior.&lt;br&gt;
This is one of only a handful of prints depicting the historical character Ōishi Kuranosuke rather than an actor using a thinly veiled character name, e.g. Ōboshi Yuranosuke, playing Ōishi in a kabuki version of the historical incident.&lt;br&gt;
This print is a re-design of the print as originally issued. (See this collection&#39;s print IHL.0503.) The blocks for the original design must have been damaged or lost. This re-designed print kept the same print number in the series, the same publication date, and the same descriptive scroll.
