A depiction of the wounded Chinese Commander Tso Pao-kwei and other Chinese soldiers being led into a Japanese field hospital for treatment after the fall of Pyongyang in Korea to the Japanese forces on September 16, 1894.  While some accounts of the battle reported that Tso Pao-kwei had surrendered, in fact he fought bravely and died in action.&lt;br&gt; 
&lt;br&gt;For another impression of this print see IHL.0571.
This set of prints is unusal in its being  published in Ōsaka rather than in Tokyo where almost all Sino-Japanese War prints were published. Its publisher Ōbuchi Wataru of the publishing house Hatsubai Shinshindō was primarily a book publisher and this print was part of a twelve print gafu (picture book), a portfolio titled Sino-Japanese War Picture Book.

While the title does not mention the name of the bugler being depicted in this print, it must be the storied Army Private Kiguchi Kohei, who was hit by an enemy bullet and died with his bugle still at his lips during the battle of Songhwan on July 24, 1894. The strange story behind this famous bugler is that he was first misidentified as Shirakami Genjirō, another soldier, but not a bugler, who had died in the same battle.The error did not become clear until a funeral for Shirakami with over 400 guests had taken place.&lt;br&gt;
Once the true name of the heroic bugler became clear, he would enter the pantheon of heroes who gave their lives for the Emperor and Japan.&lt;br&gt;
This set of prints is unusal in its being  published in Ōsaka rather than in Tokyo where almost all Sino-Japanese War prints were published. Its publisher Ōbuchi Wataru of the publishing house Hatsubai Shinshindō was primarily a book publisher and this print was part of a twelve print gafu (picture book), a portfolio titled Sino-Japanese War Picture Book.

A depiction of captured Chinese sailors from the gunboat Tsao Kiang (Caojiang) which was captured off Asan, Korea by the Japanese cruiser Akitsushima on July 27, 1894 during the Battle of Pungdo, the first naval battle of the Sino-Japanese War (1894-1895). The sailors are likely being transferred to the Japanese dispatch vessel Yaeyama.  Conflicting information exists on the history of the Tsao Kiang and also its renaming as a ship of the Japanese navy, with most sources (including the title of this print) listing its new Japanese name as &quot;Sōkō&quot; and at least one source listing its new name as the &quot;Toyshina.&quot; 
&lt;br&gt;For another impression of this print see IHL.0570.
&lt;br&gt;This set of prints is unusal in its being  published in Ōsaka rather than in Tokyo where almost all Sino-Japanese War prints were published. Its publisher Ōbuchi Wataru of the publishing house Hatsubai Shinshindō was primarily a book publisher and this print was part of a twelve print gafu (picture book), a portfolio titled Sino-Japanese War Picture Book.

A lithograph from a photographic portrait of Edward Prince of Wales, later King Edward VIII (1894-1972), appearing in the Tokyo Asahi Graphic (東京朝日グラフィック). The caption beneath the portrait reads, &quot;His Royal Highness, the Crown Prince of the United Kingdom, who is visiting soon.&quot;
&lt;br&gt;
&quot;By the time the Prince of Wales arrived in Japan [in April 1922], the Emperor Taishō (1879-1926) had retired from his royal duties due to ill health. Thus, the prince was mainly entertained by the Empress Teimei and the Prince Regent [Crown Prince Hirohito]. Many press images of the visit . . . demonstrate the warm hospitality he received. However, the Prince of Wales could communicate with the empress only through an interpreter, and even then their conversations were limited to subjects such as the weather and the cherry blossom.&quot; - Source: website of the Royal Collection Trust https://www.rct.uk/collection/themes/publications/japan-courts-and-culture/visit-of-edward-prince-of-wales-later-king [accessed 8-17-23]

One of twelve prints that make up the portfolio Scenes of China published in 1936 by the Tokyo publisher Ōtsuka Minoru whose firm Ōtsuka Kōgeisha specialized in collotype reproduction. For these prints it appears that the collotype backgrounds have been overlaid with woodblock printing for the foreground to produce exquisite reproductions of designs by the Kyoto nihonga artist Takeuchi Seihō (1864-1942). This print portfolio produced towards the end of Seihō&#39;s career, reproduced his original ink on silk paintings created after his two trips to China in 1920 and 1921. Seihō&#39;s visits to China were seminal in evolving his style, as was his visit to Europe ten years earlier.&lt;br&gt;
When Seihō visited China in the early 1920s, China was still suffering under the Unequal Treaties foisted upon it beginning in 1842 which obligated China, under military threat, to offer concessions to foreign powers, essentially ceding territorial control of important ports and cities to a host of European imperialist powers, Russia, the United States and Imperial Japan. Many of the areas visited by Seihō to sketch and photograph were under Japanese control at that time, which they would maintain until their WWII defeat in 1943.&lt;br&gt;
The area visited by Seihō is known as Jiangnan (lit. “South of the [Yangtze] River&quot;) and is comprised of present-day central and southern Jiangsu province, Shanghai, Zhejiang province, southern Anhui province and some regions in Jiangxi province. Cities that fall under this definition include Suzhou and Nanjing [and Zhenjiang] in Jiangsu, Hangzhou and Shaoxing in Zhejiang, Huangshan and Anqing in Anhui, and Wuyuan in Jiangxi.&quot;

