An illustration of the stage set for the play Ichi-no-tani Futaba Gunki  (Chronicle of the Battle of Ichi-no-tani) a five-act bunraku play written by Namiki Sōsuke which premiered in 1751. The Battle of Ichi-no-tani was fought between the Minamoto (Genji) and Heike (Taira) clans during the Genpei War (1180-1185). The battle was key in the Minamoto clan&#39;s establishing the Kamakura Shogunate in 1192.&lt;br&gt;

This stage set pictures the Taira&#39;s camp in the foreground with the battleships of the Heike in the background. It will be the setting for the battle between the young Heike warrior Taira Atsumori and the Genji warrior Kumagai Jirō Naozane.&lt;br&gt;

As originally issued, this print is complete with its tissue overlay showing the outlines of three puppet characters as they appear on stage.

&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;The Illustrated Collection of Famous Japanese Puppets of the Osaka Bunrakuza&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;
The Illustrated Collection of Famous Japanese Puppets of the Osaka Bunrakuza consists of twelve sets of prints each set depicting a specific play in the bunraku repertory. Each set contains a stage set for the specific play along with three prints of puppet characters appearing in the play and an explanatory sheet, for a total of forty-eight color woodblock prints and twelve monochrome explanatory sheets. All of the plays pictured were performed at the Bunraku Theater (Bunraku-za) located within the Goryō Shrine compound in Osaka. The Goryō Bunraku-za burned down in November 1926 after what has been described as &quot;an extremely difficult managerial era&quot; and &quot;lost interest&quot; by the public in the late Taishō era. It is unknown what role, if any, the management of the Goryō Bunraku-za may have played in the formulation and issuance of this print series in their efforts to revitalize the theater.

Picturing the puppet character Tamaori Hime, Atsumori&#39;s wife, in the play Ichi-no-tani Futaba Gunki.&lt;br&gt;

&lt;b&gt;The Illustrated Collection of Famous Japanese Puppets of the Osaka Bunrakuza&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;
